/-!
# The windows avx_x4 loop cuts for the second and later passes: the saved `xmm` stripped

The windows kernel saves the callee-saved `xmm6..15` in its prologue (160 data bytes at
`sAt (-25) .. sAt (-184)`), so from the body head `K2` on every pilot cut carries those cells
at the (oldest) end of its write log.  A loop invariant head must have an empty log
(`mapState_reached`: the turn's end, stripped of its log, is the head under the substitution),
which the pass-1 cuts cannot provide.

For the second and later passes the cuts are therefore *stripped* of those 160 cells
(`strip160`, `stripKW`), and the cells move into the symbolic initial memory's push list
(`pushCW`, `m0WP := mem0qW 4 roC pushCW`) as *address-keyed* data bytes
(`.dat (.var (.m rStack (sAt k))) j`, `pushKeyed`): at the concrete level they are in the log
the first pass accumulated, and the push clause of `WfMemW rodata pushCW` holds of the viewed
memory under a memory-keyed environment because the entry slice wrote exactly those bytes and
nothing later overwrites them (every later write is an output cell or a frame cell below
`sAt (-184)`).  The body never reads them; the exit path (`KTEs`) restores `xmm6..15` from
them, which the symbolic executor now serves from the push list, so the restored registers
denote the saved bytes.

`KHs` is the stripped invariant head (`K2` with the loop-carried `xmm0..15`, the dead `rax`
and `r9` fixed to the tail's values, as the linux `AvxX4LoopHead.KHL`), `KH3s` the second
replayed cut (`K3` with the still-unwritten `xmm6..15`), `KTs` the turn's end and `KTEs` the
exit, all over `m0WP`; the pass-2 body slices are generated by `tools/winx4w.py --pass2`
(`WinAvxX4WBodyS*`).
-/

namespace Hashtree.Proofs.WinAvxX4WStrip

/-! ## The saved bytes as a push list -/

/-- The byte index of the save at `sAt (-25 - i)`: the saves run downwards, `3, 2, 1, 0`. -/
def saveByte (i : Nat) : Nat := 3 - i % 4

/-- The saved `xmm6..15`: 160 address-keyed data bytes at `sAt (-25 - i)`, `i < 160`, byte
